2 cups mini pretzels or broken pretzel crisps</li><li>1/2 cup semisweet chocolate chips</li><li>1/4 cup heavy cream</li><li>1/3 cup creamy peanut butter</li><li>2 tbsp powdered sugar</li><li>2–4 tbsp milk</li><li>1/3 cup caramel sauce</li><li>Vanilla frozen custard</li></ul></div><div><h3>Step-by-step</h3><ol><li>Heat oven to 400°F. Cut tortillas into triangles.</li><li>Brush with melted butter and sprinkle with cinnamon sugar. Bake 7–10 minutes, flipping once, until crisp.</li><li>Make chocolate sauce by pouring hot cream over chocolate chips; wait 2 minutes, then stir smooth.</li><li>Whisk peanut butter with powdered sugar and enough milk to make a pourable sauce.</li><li>Spread cinnamon chips and pretzels across a large platter.</li><li>Drizzle with warm chocolate, peanut-butter sauce and caramel.</li><li>Add several small scoops of frozen custard immediately before serving so everyone can scoop a little with the crunchy pieces.</li></ol></div></div><div class="note"><strong>KaySo note:</strong> Keep the custard in small scoops distributed around the tray instead of one giant melting mound in the middle.</div><div class="recipe-actions"><button data-share-recipe>↗ Share Recipe</button><button data-copy-recipe>Copy Recipe</button><span class="status"></span></div></div></details><details class="recipe" id="wisconsin-frozen-custard-mud-pie" data-title="Wisconsin Frozen Custard Mud Pie"><summary><span class="recipe-title">Wisconsin Frozen Custard Mud Pie</span><span class="open-label">Open Recipe ↓</span></summary><div class="recipe-body"><div class="meta"><div><span>Serves</span>6</div><div><span>Total Time</span>30 minutes + freezing</div><div><span>Difficulty</span>Easy</div><div><span>Equipment</span>9-inch pie plate; freezer.</div></div><div class="makeahead"><strong>Make-ahead:</strong> Ideal 1–3 days ahead.</div><div class="cols"><div><h3>Ingredients